-- Appendix: correcting the cross-runtime line above --
The '-> DIVERGENCE' verdict in section [1] is an artifact of MY comparison, not
a product divergence: it hashed RAW bytes across runtimes, and the two runtimes
differ in JSON whitespace/line endings by design. The repository's own parity
oracle (tests/component-path-ownership-parity.tests.sh) compares CANONICAL JSON
via 'jq -S -c'. Re-measured on that basis:
  canonical py  without --json = 046b65e328a096b8be59df98a3598ead2cf3d0718c07e27e2f594626d547dc80
  canonical py  with    --json = 046b65e328a096b8be59df98a3598ead2cf3d0718c07e27e2f594626d547dc80
  canonical ps1 without -Json  = 046b65e328a096b8be59df98a3598ead2cf3d0718c07e27e2f594626d547dc80
  canonical ps1 with    -Json  = 046b65e328a096b8be59df98a3598ead2cf3d0718c07e27e2f594626d547dc80
  -> all four identical: the flag is an accepted no-op AND cross-runtime parity holds

  Raw-byte no-op claim (the one that matters for 'no-op'), per runtime:
    python3: with == without  -> yes
    pwsh   : with == without  -> yes
